  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington is performing well against the calendar and showing real momentum year-to-date, but yesterday's 12% revenue miss was Juneteenth-driven \u2014 the holiday depressed bookings roughly as expected. Stripping that out, your DOW-adjusted performance sits at \u20131.1%, which is essentially flat. Month-to-date you're up 10% on revenue and 13% on appointments versus May, a genuine operational win. Year-to-date growth of 51% in revenue on 53% more appointments is the story: you're scaling. Yesterday's $478/groomer is 5% below your 30-day baseline of $505, but that's noise in a single day; your team is running hot across the month at 4.2 groomer-days daily versus 3.8 last month.\n\nHicksville is the watch. Yesterday's 20% revenue drop looks worse than it is \u2014 after DOW adjustment you're actually +9.4% month-to-date, which is solid. But your visit-cadence data is flashing yellow: the 12+ week gap bucket grew from 8.4% to 10.2% in recent weeks, signaling customers are stretching intervals. That's stealth churn. Add-ons underperformed at Hicksville (31% attach versus 27% last week) while retail popped 43%, suggesting front-of-house focus is mixed. The good news: you're $420/groomer yesterday versus a 30-day baseline of $389, so your three groomers are punching above their weight.\n\nOne operational concern across both stores: Hicksville has zero bather hours scheduled today and tomorrow, and only 9 groomer-days over the next week \u2014 that's skeletal. Port Washington's tomorrow is also bare (zero groomers, one day out). This weekend coverage gap will bottleneck revenue.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 3 groomers + 8.0 bather hours today, zero groomers tomorrow; Hicksville has 1 groomer today, zero tomorrow; next 7 days: 39 combined groomer-days across both locations.**"
